1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,319
Spaceflight begins with a decision.

2
00:00:03,319 --> 00:00:07,473
This is Kinetic Core, created by Mykyta
Borisov.

3
00:00:07,473 --> 00:00:09,900
Explore the Solar System.

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:14,115
Compare worlds, choose a destination, and
plan your mission.

5
00:00:14,115 --> 00:00:16,554
Then build the spacecraft.

6
00:00:16,554 --> 00:00:22,437
Select compatible components, adjust their
dimensions, and watch mass, thrust, and

7
00:00:22,437 --> 00:00:23,944
fuel change together.

8
00:00:23,944 --> 00:00:26,775
These numbers are part of the flight
model.

9
00:00:26,775 --> 00:00:32,044
Launch, manage your engine, and follow the
instruments as the mission unfolds.

10
00:00:32,044 --> 00:00:36,909
Gravity, propellant flow, and changing
mass shape every maneuver.

11
00:00:36,909 --> 00:00:40,831
For the final approach, practice a local
Moon landing.

12
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:45,255
Read altitude, vertical speed, and
sideways drift.

13
00:00:45,255 --> 00:00:49,029
Powered guidance helps, but it still uses
fuel.

14
00:00:49,029 --> 00:00:52,449
Then switch to a separate docking
exercise.

15
00:00:52,449 --> 00:00:57,369
Change camera views, align the spacecraft,
and make small corrections with

16
00:00:57,369 --> 00:00:59,164
reaction-control thrusters.

17
00:00:59,164 --> 00:01:05,400
Finally, review the touchdown: real
contact speeds, remaining fuel, and a
clear

18
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:05,953
result.

19
00:01:05,953 --> 00:01:10,965
Built for curious students and future
explorers, Kinetic Core turns engineering

20
00:01:10,965 --> 00:01:14,581
choices into something you can see, test,
and understand.

21
00:01:14,581 --> 00:01:16,885
Design the spacecraft.

22
00:01:16,885 --> 00:01:19,238
Fly the critical moments.
